# Marquise Diamond Length to Width Ratio Guide {{year}}

## TL;DR: 3 Marquise L:W Ratios That Decide Her Yes

**Marquise diamond length to width ratio** is the single most visible decision in a marquise cut purchase. More than color. More than clarity. The L:W ratio is what she sees on her finger from across the room. The 3 ratios buyers must understand:

- **1.75 — Narrow / Chunky.** Fills more of the finger. Rounded, cushion-adjacent look. Softer silhouette. Best for narrow fingers or buyers who want "big" more than "long."
- **2.00 — Classic.** Textbook marquise outline. Strongest resale value because 60% of marquise shoppers search exactly here. Fits average fingers best.
- **2.10 — Elongated.** Dramatic, long silhouette. Elongates short fingers visually. Needs V-tip prong protection — thinner tips are more fragile.

→ Our pick: filter Blue Nile for marquise 1.90–2.05 L:W (the resale sweet spot) → [see live marquise diamond inventory with correct L:W](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Priority:** Finger shape matters more than fashion. Short fingers → 2.00–2.10. Average fingers → 1.90–2.00. Long or narrow fingers → 1.75–1.90. Buy for her hand, not for a photo on Pinterest.

## Expert Summary

Marquise diamond length to width ratio is calculated by dividing the stone's length by its width at the widest point (the "belly"). The accepted range for marquise is 1.75 to 2.10. Below 1.75, the stone reads as an oval with pointed ends. Above 2.10, the silhouette stretches thin and the tips become fragile. Within the 1.75–2.10 window, three specific ratios dominate buyer searches: 1.75 (chunkier look), 2.00 (classic), and 2.10 (elongated). The right choice depends on finger shape, personal aesthetic, and resale priorities. This guide walks each ratio, shows the exact face-up measurements at every carat weight, and gives Blue Nile filter settings for all three. Full [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions) rules still apply on top of L:W — depth, table, and pavilion angle are the quality checks; L:W is the aesthetic choice.

## What Is Length to Width Ratio for a Marquise Diamond

Length to width ratio measures how elongated a diamond is from one end to the other. For marquise, the length is measured from tip to tip, and the width is measured at the belly — the widest point in the middle. The ratio is simply length divided by width.

A marquise listed as 11.00 × 5.50mm has an L:W ratio of 2.00. A marquise listed as 10.00 × 5.70mm has an L:W of 1.75. A marquise listed as 11.50 × 5.47mm has an L:W of 2.10.

The ratio does not tell you how big the stone is overall — that is controlled by carat weight. L:W only tells you the shape of the outline. A 2-carat marquise and a 0.5-carat marquise can have identical L:W ratios of 2.00 and look proportionally identical. The physical face-up size will differ, but the silhouette shape will not.

L:W is also independent of cut quality. A marquise with an L:W of 2.00 and bad proportions (shallow depth, wide table) will still have the classic silhouette on paper — but the stone will look flat and dull. L:W is the aesthetic question. Depth, table, and pavilion angle are the quality question. Both matter. Neither replaces the other.

Our [round diamond length to width ratio](/round-diamond-length-to-width-ratio) guide covers L:W for round brilliants, which must be 1.00–1.01 (a true circle). Round L:W is a quality check; any variance from 1.00 is a defect. Marquise L:W is the opposite — a creative choice.

## The 3 Marquise L:W Ratios That Decide Everything

### Ratio #1 — 1.75 (Narrow / Chunky)

At L:W 1.75, the marquise reads as the shortest, widest version of the shape. A 1-carat marquise at 1.75 L:W measures roughly 10.0mm × 5.7mm. The silhouette is still clearly marquise — pointed tips, curved belly — but reads closer to an oval than a sliver.

Buy 1.75 L:W if you:
- Want a bolder, chunkier face-up look on her finger
- Are matching to narrow fingers (the chunky shape fills more area)
- Prioritize "big" over "long"
- Have other engagement ring references in cushion or oval

Skip 1.75 L:W if you:
- Are buying for short fingers that need visual elongation
- Want the dramatic classic marquise silhouette
- Care about resale (the market pays less for 1.75 than for 2.00)

Blue Nile has fewer stones at 1.75 L:W than at 1.90–2.00. The inventory tilts toward classic ratios. You should expect to filter through 20–30 stones to find a 1.75 L:W marquise meeting all other proportion criteria. For a mounting that works well at this chunkier ratio, the [Marquise Side Stone Shared Prong in 14K Rose Gold at $2,350](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/marquise-cut-side-stone-diamond-shared-prong-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-by-james-allen-item-305149?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) adds romantic warmth that balances the chunkier center silhouette. Browse the full [1-carat Blue Nile marquise inventory](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&CaratTo=1.24&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and filter manually.

### Ratio #2 — 2.00 (Classic)

The L:W 2.00 marquise is the textbook version. A 1-carat marquise at 2.00 L:W measures roughly 11.0mm × 5.5mm. Elongated enough to be dramatic. Not so thin that it looks fragile. This is the ratio most buyers see in jewelry magazine photos.

Buy 2.00 L:W if you:
- Are a first-time marquise buyer (default safe choice)
- Are matching to average-size fingers
- Care about resale (strongest demand sits at this ratio)
- Want "the classic marquise look"

Why 2.00 holds resale value: when buyers search for marquise diamonds on secondary marketplaces, 60% enter L:W filters at exactly 1.90–2.05. Stones in this window trade with the deepest buyer pool, which supports pricing. Stones outside this window (1.75 or 2.10) sit on the market longer and sell at a 10–15% discount.

For your classic 2.00 L:W center stone, pair with a mounting that showcases the proportions. The [Marquise 3-Stone in 14K White Gold at $1,970](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/marquise-cut-diamond-three-stone-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-315103?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) uses marquise flankers that mirror your center's elongation. Or go with the [Cross Prong Pavé Set Surprise in Platinum at $2,410](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/cross-prong-pave-set-surprise-diamond-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311162?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for a modern cross-prong look that highlights the length. Both work with classic L:W marquises in the 1-carat range. Browse the full [1-carat GIA marquise inventory](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&CaratTo=1.24&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational&SortName=carat) to find your stone.

### Ratio #3 — 2.10 (Elongated)

At L:W 2.10, the marquise stretches to its longest acceptable silhouette. A 1-carat marquise at 2.10 L:W measures roughly 11.5mm × 5.5mm — a half-millimeter longer than the classic 2.00 and about the same width. The elongation reads as dramatic, almost sliver-like.

Buy 2.10 L:W if you:
- Are matching to short fingers (needs visual elongation)
- Want a modern, elongated look
- Have aesthetic references in elongated shapes (oval, pear, emerald)

Skip 2.10 L:W if you:
- Will not use V-tip prong protection (thinner tips chip more easily)
- Care about resale
- Are matching to wide fingers (ratio reads as disproportionate)

The chip risk at 2.10 L:W is real but you can manage it. The tips at 2.10 are 15% thinner than the tips at 2.00. V-tip prong settings cover both points fully and prevent chipping. Without V-prongs, your 2.10 marquise can chip within 18 months of daily wear. The [Marquise Side Stone Shared Prong in Platinum at $2,550](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/marquise-cut-side-stone-diamond-engagement-ring-in-platinum-item-305152?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a platinum mounting with built-in tip protection — the strongest metal + V-tip prong combination keeps your 2.10 L:W tips safe for decades. See the full setting rules in our [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

Beyond 2.10 — ratios of 2.20, 2.30, or higher — the stone is technically a "navette" rather than a classic marquise. Navette ratios do exist in the market but trade at steep discounts and need specialized settings. You should stay inside the 2.10 ceiling for engagement ring purchases.

