## Lab vs Natural L:W Patterns

Lab-grown marquise diamonds come from the same cutting houses as natural, but the ratio distributions differ slightly in the Blue Nile inventory.

Natural marquise inventory tilts toward classic ratios (1.95–2.05) because the demand side pulls there. Cutters cut natural rough to the ratios that sell fastest, and the market demands classic. About 50% of natural marquise inventory sits in 1.95–2.05, 20% sits in 1.75–1.94, and 30% sits in 2.05–2.15.

Lab marquise inventory is more evenly distributed. Because lab rough is cheaper and the cutting houses can afford to serve smaller demand pockets, you find more variety at the extreme ratios. About 35% of lab marquise sits in 1.95–2.05, 25% in 1.75–1.94, and 40% in 2.05–2.15.

Practical application: if you specifically want a 2.10 L:W elongated marquise, lab inventory gives you more options. If you want a classic 2.00 L:W marquise, natural inventory gives you more options.

> **Farzana's Verdict:** The ratio flexibility in lab inventory is one of the under-discussed advantages of lab-grown. If a buyer wants a marquise outside the classic ratio range — say 1.80 or 2.12 — the natural market can take weeks to find a stone meeting all other proportion criteria. The lab market usually has 5 or 6 options within a day. For buyers with specific L:W preferences, lab is the practical choice even before the price advantage comes in.

## Where the 2.00 Classic L:W Came From — A Short History

The marquise cut dates to 1745. King Louis XV of France commissioned the shape to echo the lip shape of his mistress, Jean Antoinette Poisson, the Marquise de Pompadour. The original stones were cut at L:W ratios between 1.75 and 2.00. There was no fixed standard — each cutter chose based on the rough.

The 2.00 L:W became "classic" in the 1920s. Art Deco jewelry designers standardized elongated shapes to match the geometric aesthetic of the era, and the 2.00 ratio became the default marquise outline for the next 70 years. By the 1990s, the diamond cutting industry had collectively settled on 1.90–2.05 as the "accepted" marquise range because that is what consumers asked for.

Modern cutters cut to the ratios buyers search for. The 1.95–2.05 range dominates inventory because 60% of marquise buyers filter in that window. The 1.75 and 2.10 extremes exist but are produced in smaller volumes. If buyer taste shifted — say toward 2.20 navette ratios — the inventory would follow within 2 years.

This history matters for one reason: there is no absolute "right" L:W for marquise. The 1.75–2.10 range is a market convention, not a physics requirement. A 2.15 L:W marquise cuts fire the same as a 2.00 L:W marquise if the pavilion angle is correct. The range exists because that is where buyers currently look. If she loves a 2.15 L:W stone, buy it — just know you are shopping outside the main market window and should expect fewer options and a slight resale discount.

## Comparison with Other Fancy Shapes — L:W Rules by Shape

Each fancy shape has its own accepted L:W range. Here is how they compare.

| Shape | Accepted L:W range | Classic L:W | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| Marquise | 1.75 – 2.10 | 2.00 | Widest aesthetic range of any fancy shape |
| Oval | 1.35 – 1.50 | 1.40 | Tighter window; variance less visible |
| Pear | 1.45 – 1.75 | 1.55 | Classic pear sits between oval and marquise elongation |
| Emerald | 1.30 – 1.50 | 1.40 | Step cut; L:W is both aesthetic and quality marker |
| Radiant | 1.00 – 1.50 | 1.20 | Can be square or rectangular; wide range |
| Cushion | 1.00 – 1.20 | 1.05 | Nearly square; small L:W variance |
| Princess | 1.00 – 1.05 | 1.00 | Must be square; variance outside 1.00–1.05 is a defect |
| Heart | 0.95 – 1.10 | 1.00 | Width = length; tolerances allow slight variance |

**Verdict:** Marquise has the widest acceptable L:W range of any fancy shape (0.35 range from 1.75 to 2.10). This is why L:W choice matters more for marquise than for any other shape — the same carat weight produces visibly different stones. For a shape-by-shape comparison, see our [diamond shapes guide](/diamond-shapes-guide), the [marquise vs emerald cut diamond](/marquise-cut-vs-emerald-cut-diamond) comparison, and the [round diamond vs marquise](/round-diamond-vs-marquise) analysis for the elongation trade-off.

## How L:W Interacts with Other Proportions

L:W is one of five proportion decisions on a marquise. The others — depth, table, pavilion angle, girdle — interact with L:W in ways most buyers do not realize.

**L:W + depth:** An elongated marquise (2.10 L:W) needs slightly higher depth (60–62%) to maintain brilliance. A chunky marquise (1.75 L:W) can tolerate lower depth (59–60%) without losing fire. The reason is light path length — the longer the stone, the more depth it needs to bounce light back correctly.

**L:W + table:** An elongated marquise (2.10) does better with a narrower table (54–55%). A chunky marquise (1.75) can tolerate a wider table (56–58%). Wide tables kill fire more on elongated stones because the proportional distance between facets changes.

**L:W + pavilion angle:** This interaction is the most important. An elongated marquise (2.10) with a wrong pavilion angle shows bow-tie more severely than a chunky marquise (1.75) with the same angle. The elongation amplifies the dark central line. If you are buying an elongated marquise, demand pavilion angle 40.8–41.4° (tightest acceptable window), not just 40.6–41.8°.

**L:W + girdle:** The thinner-tipped elongated marquises (2.10) are more vulnerable at the points. Avoid Thin girdles on 2.00+ L:W stones unless the setting uses V-tip prongs. Medium or Slightly Thick girdle gives more protection at the tips.

For a full walk-through of each proportion and how they combine, see our dedicated [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions) and the broader [marquise cut diamond buying framework](/marquise-cut-diamond).

> **Farzana's Verdict:** The biggest mistake buyers make with L:W is treating it as the only decision. A 2.00 L:W marquise with a 40.3° pavilion angle (below The Fire Floor) looks worse than a 2.10 L:W marquise with a correct 41.0° pavilion. L:W is your aesthetic choice. Depth, table, pavilion, and girdle are your quality checks. Get the quality right first. Then pick your L:W. If you have to compromise on one, compromise on L:W — a quality stone at 1.90 L:W outperforms a poorly-cut stone at 2.00 L:W every time.

