| 2.00 (classic) | [Cross Prong Pavé 14K WG](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/cross-prong-pave-set-surprise-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-311166?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | 14K WG | $1,710 | Cross-prong design showcases textbook marquise length | Best value modern mounting under $2,000 |
| 2.00 (classic) | [Cross Prong Pavé 14K YG](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/cross-prong-pave-set-surprise-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-311167?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | 14K YG | $1,710 | Same mounting, warm metal — lets you drop to H color | Pairs well with H-color classic marquise |
| 2.10 (elongated) | [Graduated Milgrain Platinum](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/graduated-milgrain-diamond-engagement-ring-in-platinum-1-10-ct-tw-item-194982?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Platinum | $2,220 | Platinum durability protects the thinner elongated tips | Best long-term mounting for 2.10 L:W |
| 2.10 (elongated) | [Men's Bypass Channel](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/mens-bypass-channel-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-1-4-ct-tw-item-146873?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | 14K WG | $2,085 | Channel-set side stones protect tip zones | Secure modern option for 2.10 centers |

**Verdict:** For your 2.00 L:W classic marquise, the Cross Prong Pavé at $1,710 gives you the best modern mounting at the lowest price point. For a chunkier 1.75 L:W, drop to the Marquise Accents Pavé Basket at $1,250 — you need less visual complexity to balance the shorter outline. For an elongated 2.10 L:W, upgrade to the Graduated Milgrain Platinum at $2,220 — the platinum metal and secure tip protection are worth the extra $500 to keep your thinner tips safe. Total ring budget = your mounting + your center stone; a complete 1-carat ring from this list lands in the $3,200-$4,900 range.

## Final Verdict: Best Marquise L:W by Buyer Scenario

**Best for the surprise proposal buyer (unknown finger shape)**
Default to L:W 1.95–2.00. This is the widest-fitting ratio across all finger shapes. If her fingers turn out short, this ratio still elongates them. If her fingers turn out long, this ratio still looks balanced. Safest "I do not know her exact finger shape" default. → [See the 1-carat 1.95-2.00 L:W marquise inventory on Blue Nile](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&CaratTo=1.24&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for the short-finger buyer**
Push to L:W 2.05–2.10 for maximum visual elongation. Pair with a thin band (1.5–1.8mm) to amplify the elongation effect. Insist on V-tip prongs — the thinner tips at 2.10 L:W need the protection. → [See 2.05+ L:W Blue Nile marquise inventory](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for the resale-conscious buyer**
Stay inside L:W 1.95–2.00. This is where 60% of marquise resale demand lives. A 1.5-carat GIA marquise at 2.00 L:W holds resale value 15% better than the same stone at 1.75 or 2.10. Combine the classic ratio with F-G color and VS1-VS2 clarity for maximum liquidity. → [See the 1.5 carat marquise inventory meeting resale criteria](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.50&CaratTo=1.74&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Best for the bold-aesthetic buyer**
If she loves cushion and oval shapes, go with L:W 1.75–1.85 for a chunkier marquise that reads closer to those references. Pair with a wider band (2.5–3mm) and a bezel setting for a modern, substantial look. Reject anything above 1.90 L:W — it will look wrong against her other jewelry references. → [See the chunky 1.75-1.85 L:W marquise options](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds?Shape=marquise-cut&CaratFrom=1.00&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the best length to width ratio for a marquise diamond?

The best length to width ratio for a marquise diamond is 1.95–2.00 for most buyers. This classic range has the deepest Blue Nile inventory, the strongest resale value, and fits average fingers best. Deviate from 1.95–2.00 only if finger shape or personal preference clearly calls for it — short fingers prefer 2.00–2.10 for elongation; long fingers prefer 1.80–1.90 for balance.

### Is 1.75 L:W too short for a marquise?

No, 1.75 is the lowest acceptable L:W for marquise but it is still inside the correct range. Below 1.75, the stone starts to look like an oval with pointed ends — the silhouette loses the marquise identity. At exactly 1.75, the shape is chunkier and more rounded, which some buyers prefer. The trade-off is slightly fewer options in Blue Nile's inventory and a 10% price discount vs classic 2.00.

### Is 2.10 L:W too long for a marquise?

No, 2.10 is the upper acceptable limit for marquise. Above 2.10, the stone technically becomes a "navette" rather than a marquise, and trades at a discount with fewer setting options. At exactly 2.10, the elongation is dramatic but the tips are 15% thinner than at 2.00 L:W. V-tip prong settings are mandatory to prevent chipping with daily wear.

### How does L:W affect face-up size on a marquise?

At the same carat weight, L:W changes the length and width proportionally. A 1-carat marquise at 1.75 L:W measures about 10.0mm × 5.7mm. At 2.00 L:W it measures 11.0mm × 5.5mm. At 2.10 L:W it measures 11.5mm × 5.5mm. The length difference between the extremes is 1.5mm — visually obvious on the finger.

### Does L:W affect bow-tie risk on marquise?

Yes, slightly. Elongated marquises (L:W 2.00+) show bow-tie more visibly when the pavilion angle is wrong, because the elongation amplifies any dark central area. Chunky marquises (1.75) can mask minor bow-tie better due to the shorter central span. However, L:W does not cause bow-tie — pavilion angle does. L:W determines how visible a bow-tie becomes when it exists.

### What L:W should I pick if I do not know her finger size?

Default to L:W 2.00. It is the safest choice across the widest range of finger shapes. Short fingers still get elongation. Average fingers get the classic look. Long fingers get balance (though they may prefer 1.90). The 2.00 L:W is the "no regrets" ratio for surprise proposals.

### Do lab-grown marquise diamonds have the same L:W options as natural?

Yes. Lab-grown marquises come from the same cutting houses as natural and are cut to the same L:W ranges. The inventory distribution differs slightly — lab inventory is more evenly spread across the 1.75–2.10 range, while natural inventory concentrates in the 1.95–2.05 classic window. If you want an extreme L:W (1.75 or 2.10), lab inventory often has more options.

### What L:W is best for short fingers?

L:W 2.00–2.10 is best for short fingers. The elongated shape visually extends the finger by 5–8%. Pair with a thin band (1.5–1.8mm) to amplify the effect. Avoid L:W 1.75 on short fingers — the chunky shape emphasizes rather than counters the finger length.

### What L:W is best for long fingers?

L:W 1.75–1.90 is best for long fingers. The chunkier shape balances the finger length so the stone does not look exaggerated. A 2.10 L:W marquise on a long finger reads as "too much elongation" — the ratios amplify each other. If she specifically wants the long marquise look, choose 1.90 as a compromise.

### Can I resize a marquise ring if the L:W is wrong?

No. L:W is set when the stone is cut and cannot be changed. If you buy a marquise at L:W 1.75 and later wish you had bought 2.00, the only option is to sell the stone and buy a different one. This is why finger-shape fit matters so much — pick the right L:W the first time or plan to live with the choice.

### Does L:W affect the price of a marquise diamond?

Mildly. Marquises in the classic 1.95–2.05 L:W range trade at a 5–10% premium over stones outside that range because demand concentrates there. Stones at L:W 1.75 or 2.10 sell at a slight discount. The price difference is small compared to carat, color, and clarity — do not pick a worse L:W just to save money.

### What symmetry grade should I require for a marquise diamond?

Very Good or Excellent. Marquise symmetry grade controls belly placement — the widest point should sit exactly in the center. Good symmetry allows a visible 0.5mm+ belly offset which disrupts the mirror effect. At 2.00+ L:W, insist on Excellent symmetry because elongation amplifies any asymmetry.

### Should I buy a marquise with an off-center belly?

No. A belly that sits off-center from the midpoint between the tips disrupts the stone's symmetry. Even a 0.6mm offset is visible to a trained eye and reduces resale value by 15–20%. GIA symmetry grades Very Good or Excellent indicate acceptable belly placement; anything below Very Good is likely to show belly offset.

### How do I measure L:W from the Blue Nile stone page?

Blue Nile lists the measurements as length × width × depth in millimeters at the top of every stone's detail page. Divide length by width to get the L:W ratio. For example, a stone listed as 11.00 × 5.50 × 3.42mm has L:W = 11.00 / 5.50 = 2.00. Some stones display L:W explicitly on the Blue Nile page; others require the manual calculation.

